rep("A more recent example comes from the cellular industry.", '<p>The cellular industry is a more recent example. Before 2013 three or four legacy carriers ran the same playbook of subsidized handsets, two-year contracts, and hidden fees. T-Mobile broke ranks by dropping long-term contracts and betting that satisfied customers would stay—and when T-Mobile and Sprint later merged, the combined company kept the T-Mobile name because it had the stronger brand equity. The legacy carriers eventually dropped contracts too, and AT&amp;T answered with family plans, a bundled offering with retention power of its own.</p>')
rep("Firms should always try to compete \"above the line,\"", '<p>Firms should compete "above the line," by increasing benefits, rather than "below the line," by cutting price. Any firm can cut price, and competitors imitate a price cut easily; added benefits are harder to mimic, so competing above the line yields a more sustainable advantage.</p>')
rep("Interestingly, there is actually no federal law that directly regulates", '<p>No federal law directly regulates advertised reference prices; the FTC has not brought a deceptive-reference-price case since the 1970s, and the state laws that exist differ so much that they amount to a patchwork, leaving firms wide latitude.</p>')
rep("Wine is the classic case.", '<p>Wine is the classic case: quality is hard to judge from the shelf, and in blind tastings the same wine is rated worse when people are told it costs less. An fMRI study by Baba Shiv found that the same wine labeled $50 rather than $10 produced measurably more activity in the medial orbitofrontal cortex, a region thought to encode pleasure—the brain behaved as if the wine really was more enjoyable. One interpretation is that a high price makes people more mindful of the experience, and being mindful improves it.</p>')
rep("Each approach sets price to hit a particular goal", '<p>Each approach sets price to hit a particular goal. Some techniques model a direct seller–customer relationship (breakeven, target profit, and target ROI pricing); others model a channel relationship (markup, demand-minus, and chain markup pricing). Each later method is a small twist on an earlier one.</p>')
rep("The textbook presents the same relationship solved the other way around", '<p>The textbook solves the same relationship the other way around: given a price, the breakeven quantity is total fixed costs divided by the contribution per unit (price minus unit variable cost). The algebra is the same.</p>')
rep("Note that the percentage markup refers to a percentage of the selling price", '<p>The percentage markup refers to a percentage of the selling price, not of the cost: buy a garment for $50, mark it up 50%, and you sell it for $100—the $50 markup is 50% of the selling price. Whenever someone quotes a markup, ask whether it is a percentage of cost or of selling price.</p>')
rep("Conceptually, you can think of markup as the amount each sale contributes", '<p>Markup is the amount each sale contributes to operating expenses and profit; a retailer using a 50% markup charges twice what the good cost it. Markups on high-volume goods tend to be lower than on low-volume goods. Because the markup ratio is a fraction below 1, dividing cost by it always yields a price above cost.</p>')
