rep("Therefore, the price that would gain us half of the market would be $3.19.", '<p>Therefore, the parity price is $3.19. Our price is higher because our bells deliver more perceived value (214 versus 201); a brand with less value would have to price below the incumbent. To check the work, confirm the ratios are equal: 201 ÷ $3.00 ≈ 214 ÷ $3.19 ≈ 67. Lowering the price gains share; raising it loses share. A new entrant might launch a little below parity—say, $3.10—then raise the price toward $3.19 as the brand catches on: a penetration strategy.</p>')
rep("Companies sometimes engage in value-based pricing by <strong>\"unbundling\" price</strong>.", '<p>Companies sometimes engage in value-based pricing by <strong>"unbundling" price</strong>—a way to communicate a price rather than to set one, so customers see the value they receive for what they pay. It is most useful, often in B2B, when a firm is the higher-priced leader and must answer, "Why pay more?" The firm identifies every source of value it delivers relative to competitors and puts a dollar figure on each, ideally working through them with the customer:</p>')
